===Etymology===
[[w:List of pseudo-French words adapted to English|Pseudo-Gallicism]], from {{der|en|fr|double||double}} + {{m|fr|entendre||to mean, to [[understand]]}}. (The phrase is unused and ungrammatical in French. The second word, an infinitive in French, may be a corruption of the noun {{m|fr|entente}}.)

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/dubl ɑ̃tɑ̃ːdɹ/}}<ref name="OED">[http://dictionary.oed.com/cgi/entry/50068990 “double entendre”] listed in the ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' [2<sup>nd</sup> Ed.; 1989]</ref>
* {{a|UK|Anglicised}} {{IPA|en|/dʌbəl ɒnˈtɒndɹə/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-double entendre.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-double entendre.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{examples-right|examples=After {{w|Jeff Bezos}} publicly accused {{w|David Pecker}}, the owner of American Media, Inc., of “extortion and blackmail”, the ''New York Post'' opened with a headline proclaiming, [https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8e/New_York_Post_font_page_111307.jpg “BEZOS EXPOSES PECKER”]. In the US, the word “{{l|en|pecker}}” is also slang for “penis”.}}
{{en-noun|double entendres|double entendre|pl2qual=nonstandard}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} A [[phrase]] that has two [[meaning]]s, especially where one is [[innocent]] and [[literal]], the other [[risqué]], [[bawdy]], or [[ironic]]; an [[innuendo]].
#* '''1812''', ''A treatise on politeness'', tr. from the French by a lady, page 172
#*: Avoid all equivocal expressions, usually denominated '''double entendre'''; they are certain proofs of a mean and indelicate mind.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1891|author=Paulist Fathers|title=Catholic World|page=785|passage=It is a momentous crusade without the cross; and an insidious one, for the calumnies and '''double entendre''' against the church are well wrapped up and keenly distributed.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=James P. Lantolf|title=Sociocultural Theory and Second Language Learning|page=126|passage=It is not only the teacher's play with single words, phrases, and '''double entendre''' that are common in my classroom data.}}

